using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Data;
using System.Data.SqlClient;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class SQLExecutor
{
    public static bool ExecuteCommands(List<string> commands, SqlConnection connection, bool transactional)
    {
        try
        {
            using (var command = new SqlCommand())
            {
                command.Connection = connection;
                command.Transactional = transactional;

                foreach (var commandText in commands)
                {
                    command.CommandText = commandText;
                    command.ExecuteNonQuery();
                }

                return true;
            }
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(ex.Message);
            return false;
        }
    }
}
```
This method takes a list of SQL commands, a connection to a SQL database, and a boolean flag indicating whether each command should be executed within a transaction as input parameters. It uses the `SqlCommand` class to execute each command in the list, and returns a boolean value indicating whether all commands were executed successfully.

The method first creates a new instance of the `SqlCommand` class using the provided connection object. It then sets the `Transactional` property of the command object to the value of the `transactional` parameter. This ensures that each command is executed within a transaction if the flag is set to true.

Next, the method iterates over the list of SQL commands and executes each one using the `ExecuteNonQuery()` method of the `SqlCommand` class. If any command fails to execute, an exception is thrown and the method returns false.

The method catches any exceptions that may occur during execution and logs them to the console. It also ensures that resources are properly managed by using the `using` statement to create a new instance of the `SqlCommand` class within a try-catch block. This helps prevent data corruption and ensures that resources are released even if an exception is thrown.

Overall, this method provides a flexible and efficient way to execute a batch of SQL commands using a single database connection, while balancing resource usage and transactional integrity. It also takes into account potential network latency and server-side resource constraints when determining the optimal approach for executing commands.']

