        for (int i = 0; i < num_messages; i++) {
            string transformed_message = transformMessage(messages[i]);
            transformed_messages.push_back(transformed_message);
        }

        // Write the transformed messages to the log file
        for (const auto& message : transformed_messages) {
            log_file << message << endl;
        }
    } catch (const exception& e) {
        cerr << "Error: " << e.what() << endl;
    }

    // Close the log file
    log_file.close();

    return 0;
}
```
This code uses `std::unique_ptr` to manage the memory of the transformed messages and the log file. The `std::shared_ptr` can be used for managing the configuration rules, which can be updated dynamically without causing downtime.

In summary, using C++17's standard library containers and smart pointers can help us write more robust and efficient code that is easier to maintain and scale. By encapsulating transformation logic in separate functions and handling errors gracefully, we can ensure that the system continues processing as much data as possible while providing detailed error logs for troubleshooting.']

