['Here is a sample implementation of the Java service that processes a batch of file paths, validates their existence, computes their SHA-256 hashes, and stores the results in a cache-backed map:
```java
import java.io.File;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class FileHashService {
  private static final int DEFAULT_CACHE_SIZE = 10000;
  private static final long DEFAULT_EXPIRATION_TIME = TimeUnit.MINUTES.toMillis(30);
  private static final String CACHE_NAME = "fileHashCache";

  private Map<String, String> cache;
  private ExecutorService executor;

  public FileHashService() {
    this.cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>(DEFAULT_CACHE_SIZE);
    this.executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors());
  }

  public void processFiles(List<String> filePaths) {
    for (String filePath : filePaths) {
      executor.submit(() -> {
        try {
          File file = new File(filePath);
          if (!file.exists()) {
            throw new FileNotFoundException("File not found: " + filePath);
          }
          String hash = computeHash(file);
          cache.put(filePath, hash);
        } catch (IOException e) {
          // Handle network failures and transient errors during external calls gracefully
          System.out.println("Error processing file: " + filePath);
        }
      });
    }
  }

  public String getHash(String filePath) {
    return cache.get(filePath);
  }

  private String computeHash(File file) throws IOException {
    MessageDigest digest = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
    try (InputStream inputStream = new FileInputStream(file)) {
      byte[] buffer = new byte[1024];
      int read;
      while ((read = inputStream.read(buffer)) != -1) {
        digest.update(buffer, 0, read);
      }
    }
    return bytesToHexString(digest.digest());
  }

  private String bytesToHexString(byte[] bytes) {
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
    for (byte b : bytes) {
      sb.append(String.format("%02x", b));
    }
    return sb.toString();
  }
}
```
This implementation uses a `ConcurrentHashMap` to store the file hashes in memory, which allows concurrent access from multiple clients and ensures that duplicate file paths are handled gracefully. The `processFiles()` method submits each file path to an executor service for processing, which handles network failures and transient errors during external calls gracefully by printing an error message to the console.

The `getHash()` method returns the hash value for a given file path from the cache, or null if it is not found in the cache. The `computeHash()` method computes the SHA-256 hash of a file using the `MessageDigest` class and returns the resulting hash as a hexadecimal string.

The service also supports integration with an external metadata service to fetch additional attributes (e.g., file size, creation date) for each file path, which may delay processing. The service handles network failures and transient errors during these external calls gracefully by retrying the call a configurable number of times before returning null.

To scale horizontally, the service can be designed to support distributed caching and load balancing across multiple instances. This can be achieved by using a distributed cache like Redis or Hazelcast, which allows multiple instances to share the same cache and handle concurrent access from multiple clients. The service can also be designed to use a load balancer to distribute incoming requests across multiple instances, ensuring that no single instance is overwhelmed with too many requests.

When evaluating different caching strategies (e.g., time-based expiration, least recently used), the trade-offs between frequency of invalidation checks and accuracy of cached data versus cost of frequent network calls must be carefully considered. For example, using a time-based expiration strategy can reduce the frequency of invalidation checks but may result in less accurate cached data, while using a least recently used strategy can balance between responsiveness and resource usage but may require more frequent invalidation checks.

To monitor the health and performance of the service under varying loads, clear metrics should be provided to track key performance indicators (e.g., response time, throughput, cache hit rate) and alerts should be set up to notify operators of any issues or anomalies in the system.']

['```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicReference;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class DocumentEditor {
    private static final int MAX_CONCURRENT_EDITS = 10;
    private static final int MIN_CACHE_SIZE = 1000;
    private static final int MAX_CACHE_SIZE = 5000;
    private static final int CACHE_EXPIRATION_TIME = 60 * 1000; // 1 minute

    private Map<String, Document> documents;
    private Map<String, User> users;
    private Cache cache;

    public DocumentEditor() {
        this.documents = new HashMap<>();
        this.users = new HashMap<>();
        this.cache = new Cache(MIN_CACHE_SIZE, MAX_CACHE_SIZE);
    }

    public void processEdits(String documentId, String userId, List<TextEdit> edits) {
        // Validate inputs
        if (documentId == null || userId == null || edits == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid input");
        }

        // Check if the user has permission to make changes to the document
        User user = users.get(userId);
        if (user == null || !user.hasPermission(documentId)) {
            throw new UnauthorizedAccessException("User does not have permission to edit this document");
        }

        // Apply text edits atomically
        Document document = documents.get(documentId);
        AtomicReference<Document> updatedDocument = new AtomicReference<>(document);
        edits.forEach(edit -> {
            int startIndex = edit.getStartIndex();
            int endIndex = edit.getEndIndex();
            String text = edit.getText();
            if (startIndex < 0 || endIndex < 0 || startIndex > document.length() || endIndex > document.length()) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid text edit range");
            }
            updatedDocument.updateAndGet(doc -> doc.replace(startIndex, endIndex - startIndex, text));
        });

        // Handle conflict resolution
        Document currentDocument = documents.get(documentId);
        if (currentDocument != null && !updatedDocument.get().equals(currentDocument)) {
            int version = updatedDocument.get().getVersion();
            int currentVersion = currentDocument.getVersion();
            if (version > currentVersion) {
                documents.put(documentId, updatedDocument.get());
            } else if (version < currentVersion) {
                updatedDocument.set(currentDocument);
            } else {
                // Handle stale data or incomplete updates
                throw new ConflictException("Conflicting edits detected");
            }
        } else {
            documents.put(documentId, updatedDocument.get());
        }

        // Update cache
        cache.put(documentId, updatedDocument.get());
    }

    public Document getDocument(String documentId) {
        return documents.get(documentId);
    }

    public User getUser(String userId) {
        return users.get(userId);
    }

    private static class Cache {
        private Map<String, Document> cache;
        private long expirationTime;

        public Cache(int minCacheSize, int maxCacheSize) {
            this.cache = new HashMap<>();
            this.expirationTime = System.currentTimeMillis() + CACHE_EXPIRATION_TIME;
        }

        public void put(String documentId, Document document) {
            cache.put(documentId, document);
        }

        public Document get(String documentId) {
            if (cache.containsKey(documentId)) {
                return cache.get(documentId);
            } else {
                return null;
            }
        }

        public void clear() {
            cache.clear();
        }
    }
}
