To optimize performance, this implementation uses a cache strategy to store frequently accessed transactions in memory. When a new transaction is added or an existing transaction is updated, its status is stored in a cache. The cache is periodically flushed to disk to ensure that it remains small and does not consume too much memory. This allows the application to maintain a responsive performance while minimizing memory overhead.

To handle the external data source for real-time updates on transaction statuses, this implementation uses a separate thread to fetch the latest status of each transaction from the data source. The thread periodically checks if any transactions have been updated since the last time it was run and updates their status accordingly. This allows the application to maintain up-to-date information about the status of each transaction while minimizing latency and potential data inconsistencies.

To ensure that the implementation is scalable and maintainable, this implementation uses a modular design with separate functions for adding, updating, and printing transactions. Each function has a clear purpose and is responsible for a specific task. This makes it easier to add new features or modify existing ones without affecting the overall performance of the application. Additionally, the use of a linked list allows for efficient memory allocation and deallocation, while the cache strategy minimizes memory overhead while maintaining responsive performance.']

