['```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

// Define a struct to represent a task
struct Task {
    int priority;
    struct timespec deadline;
    bool is_active;
};

// Define a linked list data structure to store tasks
struct TaskList {
    struct Task *head;
    struct Task *tail;
};

// Function to add a new task to the list
void addTask(struct TaskList *list, int priority, struct timespec deadline) {
    // Create a new task node
    struct Task *new_task = malloc(sizeof(struct Task));
    new_task->priority = priority;
    new_task->deadline = deadline;
    new_task->is_active = true;

    // Add the new task to the list
    if (list->head == NULL) {
        list->head = new_task;
        list->tail = new_task;
    } else {
        list->tail->next = new_task;
        list->tail = new_task;
    }
}

// Function to update the priority of a task in the list
void updateTaskPriority(struct TaskList *list, int old_priority, int new_priority) {
    // Find the task with the specified old priority
    struct Task *task = list->head;
    while (task != NULL && task->priority != old_priority) {
        task = task->next;
    }

    // Update the priority of the found task
    if (task != NULL) {
        task->priority = new_priority;
    }
}

// Function to remove a task from the list by priority level
void removeTaskByPriority(struct TaskList *list, int priority) {
    // Find the task with the specified priority
    struct Task *task = list->head;
    while (task != NULL && task->priority != priority) {
        task = task->next;
    }

    // Remove the found task from the list
    if (task != NULL) {
        if (task == list->head) {
            list->head = task->next;
        } else {
            struct Task *prev_task = task->prev;
            prev_task->next = task->next;
        }
        free(task);
    }
}

// Function to iterate over the tasks in the list and mark expired tasks as such
void markExpiredTasks(struct TaskList *list) {
    // Iterate over the tasks in the list
    struct Task *task = list->head;
    while (task != NULL) {
        // Check if the task has passed its deadline
        if (task->deadline.tv_sec < time(NULL)) {
            // Mark the task as expired
            task->is_active = false;
        }
        task = task->next;
    }
}

// Function to efficiently iterate over the tasks in the list
void iterateTasks(struct TaskList *list, void (*callback)(struct Task *task)) {
    // Iterate over the tasks in the list
    struct Task *task = list->head;
    while (task != NULL) {
        callback(task);
        task = task->next;
    }
}
```
This code defines a `Task` struct to represent a task with priority, deadline, and status. It also defines a `TaskList` struct to store tasks in a linked list. The functions `addTask`, `updateTaskPriority`, `removeTaskByPriority`, `markExpiredTasks`, and `iterateTasks` are provided to manage the task list efficiently.

The `addTask` function adds a new task to the end of the list, while the `updateTaskPriority` function updates the priority of an existing task in the list. The `removeTaskByPriority` function removes a task from the list by its priority level. The `markExpiredTasks` function iterates over the tasks in the list and marks expired tasks as such. Finally, the `iterateTasks` function provides a way to efficiently iterate over the tasks in the list and perform an action on each task.

The code is designed with performance and scalability in mind, using a linked list data structure to store tasks for efficient insertion, deletion, and iteration. The `markExpiredTasks` function uses a simple linear search to find expired tasks, which should be efficient for small to medium-sized task lists. However, for large task lists, a more efficient data structure such as a binary search tree or a hash table may be used instead.

The code is also designed with usability and maintainability in mind, providing clear documentation and modular design to make it easy to integrate into different parts of the application and update without introducing bugs or breaking changes.']

