The function first allocates memory for the keys array using `malloc`. If this fails, it returns an error status. Then, it initializes the keys array with random values within the specified range using a loop. If a duplicate key is detected, the function retries the assignment until a unique key is found.

Next, the function allocates memory for the records array using `malloc`. If this fails, it returns an error status. Then, it populates the records array with random values and descriptive text using a loop. If any of the memory allocation operations fail, the function frees any allocated memory and returns an error status.

The function also includes a check for potential memory fragmentation using `realloc`, which can occur if the budget is too small to accommodate all the records. In this case, the function will return an error status.

Finally, the function returns a status indicating success or failure, along with a pointer to a dynamically allocated result structure containing the populated data. The caller is responsible for freeing any allocated memory using `free`.']

